<h1>Neuro Project To-Do</h1>
<p class="updated">Last updated: 2026-07-17. <b>500M v3 IS LAUNCHED.</b> torch.compile fixed and shipped (~3x, see <code>reference_torch_compile_delta.md</code> Update 2026-07-17: a real <code>lif_parallel</code> miscompile bug found+fixed, <code>reduce-overhead</code> crashed on an open upstream PyTorch bug so shipped <code>compile_mode: default</code> instead, MQAR gets its own separate compiled wrapper). <code>block_size</code> raised 512&rarr;640 via a real VRAM probe at true 500M shape (largest fitting &le;90% of the 5090's 32GB), confirmed via a 30M screen — a genuine quality lever (more context for the memory pathway), not just speed. <code>mqar_cadence=2</code> (half-frequency MQAR) was tried and REJECTED: val_ppl looked great (37.51 vs 58.09, 2-seed confirmed) but <code>recall_eval</code> showed a real recall regression at high N (N=32 56%&rarr;36%, N=64 34%&rarr;17%) — general capability improving while recall degrades is the exact failure mode this project's memory-first design watches for; left at default (every step). The <code>sparsity_lambda</code> A/B (1.5 vs 3.0 vs default 0.5) also came back negative — both higher-lambda arms cut memory-Δ substantially (0.5: 156 &rarr; 1.5: 105 &rarr; 3.0: 65) for a lower spike rate that wasn't worth the memory-pathway cost; kept default 0.5. Pre-launch audit (Fable-on-High) found and fixed 5 real issues: leftover smoke checkpoint (deleted), <code>engram_factual_eval.py</code>'s <code>build()</code> drift (also missing <code>gdn2_enabled</code>, worse than first suspected — fixed, re-confirmed Engram h4 helps), an EngramV2 prime-count assert undercount, unbounded brain-snapshot growth (<code>snapshot_brain: false</code>), and crash-resume not advancing the data RNG/curriculum counter. See <code>neuro_500m_v3_launched.md</code> memory and journey §45 for full detail. Older completions and rejected candidates live in <a href="to-do-archive.html">to-do-archive.html</a>.</p>

<h2>Currently Running</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>5090</b>: <code>500m_v3.yaml</code> — the real launch. 479M params, block_size=640, batch_size=15, grad_accum=2, compile=true (mode=default), live throughput ~11,343 tok/s, ETA ~5 days for the 5B-token target. Log: <code>logs/500m_v3.txt</code>. Resume/check: <code>tail -f ~/projects/neuro/logs/500m_v3.txt</code>. Survived one Windows-reboot kill (relaunched clean, no checkpoint lost) and one VRAM/WDDM-shared-memory throughput scare (fixed by dropping batch_size 16&rarr;15).</li>
  <li><b>5080</b>: idle.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>350M MoR is PAUSED</b> (checkpoints moved to backup drive by Glenn, out of scope). <b>200M/2B MoR test KILLED</b> by Glenn (too slow) — not relaunching; recursion work deferred to Phase 3 below.</p>

<h2>Phase 3 (future work, post-500M-v3): recursion</h2>
<p>Recursion/recurrent-depth is OFF the 500M v3 critical path. Two sub-questions, both already answered for the cheap version:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Uniform/fixed-depth looping (Huginn-style, no router): CLOSED NEGATIVE, 2-seed confirmed.</b> Fair matched-token control (depth-1 74.55/73.35, seeds 0/1) beats plain depth-2 (77.35/78.52) and depth-2+deep-supervision (79.49/76.94). See <code>neuro_adaptive_compute.md</code> for the full multi-round investigation (includes two rounds of ruling out eval-noise and token-budget confounds before reaching this verdict). Do not re-test this form.</li>
  <li><b>Per-token adaptive router (MoR proper, the paper's actual claim): UNRESOLVED, needs real engineering before it can be tested fairly.</b> Stage 2 (<code>RecurrentDepthRouter</code> in <code>src/adaptive_compute.py</code>) only ever implemented the dense/masked variant — every token gets the full extra pass computed regardless of the router's decision, so there is currently zero FLOP saving from routing. The sparse-gather Phase 2 (only compute the extra pass for tokens the router actually keeps) was scoped from day one but never built. Tonight's <code>screen_mor_200m_2b</code> ran at ~2295 tok/s (7.7 days for 2B tokens) because of this gap, and was killed by Glenn as impractical. The paper's own ablations need &gt;=135M params AND ~0.9-2.3B tokens to beat vanilla even with a working sparse implementation. <b>Next step if revisited: build the sparse-gather Phase 2 first, then re-run a MoR screen at a token budget the paper says is meaningful — not before 500M v3 ships.</b></li>
</ul>

<h2>Immediate Plan</h2>
<p><b>Since the last update (code-review pass, 2026-07-12 late-morning):</b> a deep architecture/roadmap review landed 11 findings (F1&ndash;F11), of which four were applied this session as pure additive changes safe alongside the running chains: <b>F3</b> (β pre-floor <code>at_floor_frac</code> telemetry, restores collapse-pressure visibility under <code>beta_floor: 0.1</code> where the standing "dead_frac climbing = stop" rule can never trigger by construction), <b>F11</b> (α clamp-boundary <code>hi_frac</code>/<code>lo_frac</code> telemetry to catch the analog silent-failure mode of decay-gate freeze), <b>F10</b> (added missing <code>beta</code>, <code>eta</code>, <code>ponder_*</code>, <code>recurrent_deep_supervision</code>, <code>recurrent_pass_weights</code> kwargs to <code>scripts/recall_eval.py</code>'s <code>build()</code> — same silent-wrong-arch class already bitten twice), <b>F4</b>+<b>F6</b> (design-doc amendments: SDM sizing footnote noting the 500M's fla kernel splits d_mem=2048 into 8&times;256&sup2; diagonal blocks — the "architectural ceiling" verdict was measured on ~8&times; less effective per-layer state than the notional dense count; and composition matrix updated: GDN-2/OSDN are per-slot write-rule variants of SDM, NOT mutually exclusive as originally framed).</p>
<p><b>Also landed &mdash; SDM phase 2 (biggest strategic item):</b> <code>src/sdm.py</code> built (<code>PKMIndex</code> with outer-sum + per-sub top-M candidate + global top-K, O(K&middot;&radic;N); <code>SparseSDMMemory</code> owning M_0 zero-init + separate write/read PKM heads, reusing block β/α/v so <code>beta_floor</code>/<code>decay_gate</code> telemetry stay in place). <b>Fracconn-style CPU verification 22/22:</b> PKM outer-sum invariant + top-K vs dense oracle at K&isin;{1,4,8}; SDM zero-init + &beta;=0 exact-zero (safe residual drop-in); &beta;=0 closed-form deterministic reads; &alpha;=0 fast-forget correctness; grad flow through M_0 and both PKM heads; determinism; autocast bf16 finiteness; slot-selection telemetry populated. See <code>temp/test_sdm.py</code>. Design-doc "dense_all_slots reduces to gated delta" claim corrected as aspirational-and-false; SDM's per-slot (N,d) memory is structurally different from delta's (d_v,d_k) shared-outer-product state at every parameter setting.</p>
<p><b>SDM phase 3 landed same session (2026-07-12 afternoon).</b> Re-examined the earlier deferral: model.py edits only affect FUTURE python subprocess launches (already-running processes keep their imported module in memory), and seed1 wasn't due to launch for ~3.7h &mdash; ample buffer to land + verify flag-off-safe changes first, same as the GDN-2/OSDN precedent. <code>sdm_enabled: false</code> + <code>sdm_N/sdm_W/sdm_R</code> wired into <code>SpikingHebbianBlock</code>/<code>SpikingHebbianLM</code>, config plumbing in <code>build_model</code> + <code>recall_eval.build</code>. Phase-3 scope simplification: <code>sdm_N</code> must equal <code>d_mem</code> (enforced) so the state stays square and the existing state allocator needs zero changes &mdash; matches the 30M screen's already-scoped sizing exactly; the 500M's non-square sizing needs a separate future state-allocator refactor. <b>Verified 21/21</b> in <code>temp/test_sdm_wiring.py</code>: flag-off bit-identical, flag-on 1&amp;2-layer builds/trains with finite grads, all 7 guards fire, <code>sdm_N</code> defaults to <code>d_mem</code>. <code>configs/screen_sdm_ab_treatment_30m.yaml</code> built and verified end-to-end (46.43M params vs the shared control's 44.07M, +2.36M &mdash; less than the design doc's original ~3M/layer estimate since β/α gates are reused, not duplicated). <b>Ready to launch on the next free GPU slot.</b></p>
<p><b>Findings NOT applied this session (queued):</b> <b>F1</b> (Muon <code>fix_gate_matrices</code> should be true for 500M restart forward; currently every run routes W_beta/W_alpha through Newton-Schulz which degenerates on 1-row matrices), <b>F2</b> (Engram-v2 <code>bucket.weight</code> lands in Muon not AdamW; may leave gains unclaimed, needs 30M A/B), <b>F5</b> (define 500M restart trigger to include SDM 30M verdict, not just 90M bake confirm), <b>F8</b> (confidence-gated inference replay &mdash; ranked as post-SDM), <b>F9</b> (GDN-2/OSDN chunk-fn dtype match to control if they promote to 90M).</p>
<p><b>Next actions, in order:</b></p>
<ol>
  <li><b>Review the 90M combined-bake verdict</b> when it lands (seed0 at ~52%, val_ppl 56.85 at step 12800; seed1 chained after). <b>Observation from live watch:</b> β saturated at floor (μ=0.10, floor=0.10) on all 4 blocks from ~step 6000 onward &mdash; the pre-floor sigmoid is dead-across-the-board, exactly what F3's new <code>at_floor_frac</code> telemetry is designed to make visible (this run predates the telemetry so it just shows μ=0.10 dead=0%). Run is still healthy (memory-Δ climbing +38→+48.6) but the delta-rule β modulation is functionally constant &mdash; the observed win may be more about NorMuon-optimized static delta + Engram-v2 than β-modulated composition. <b>This is the F1 pathology in the flesh</b> (Muon Newton-Schulz on the 1-row W_beta matrix produces direction-normalized constant-magnitude updates that don't respect gradient magnitude, plausibly driving W_beta into saturation). If confirmed at 90M seed1 too, the 500M restart plan is ready to draft &mdash; but the 500M config MUST set <code>muon_fix_gate_matrices: true</code> (F1) to give β a chance to actually modulate, or else the restart bakes in a known-dead gate on the thesis-critical delta rule.</li>
  <li><b>Review GDN-2 30M seed0 final &mdash; DONE, WIN.</b> Final val_ppl <b>76.98 vs control 81.87 (−4.9 ppl, well past ~1.5 ppl noise floor)</b>, memory-Δ +304.5 stable, 10.91h run. Both β blocks healthy (0.53, 0.59). Clears both bar conditions from <code>docs/gdn2_osdn_design.md</code> gate. <b>Next:</b> queue GDN-2 seed1 confirm before touching OSDN/LoRA outcomes.</li>
  <li><b>Review OSDN 30M seed0</b> (currently at step 400/6103, ~4h remaining on the 5090) and <b>LoRA SFT 30M</b> as they come off the same chain.</li>
  <li><b>SDM phase 3</b> once the 5080 seed1 subprocess is running: model.py wiring behind <code>sdm_enabled: false</code>, mutually-exclusive guards, block <code>init_state()</code> method for the (B,N,d) shape, config plumbing in <code>build_model</code> + <code>recall_eval.build()</code>, then bit-exact flag-off state_dict + forward check before any GPU. See <code>docs/sdm_design.md</code> phase 3-5.</li>
  <li><b>SDM 30M A/B</b> (screen configs, byte-identical to GDN-2/OSDN control except SDM flags) as soon as phase 3 lands and a GPU slot frees up.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Open</h2>
<ol>
  <li><b>350M replan decision (2 of 3 gate items CLEARED, waiting on Frac-connections).</b> NorMuon A/B and Engram v2 A/B are both confirmed wins. Frac-connections treatment running. When it clears: option (a) restart from the paused checkpoint with <code>beta_floor</code> — recovers memory but keeps other pre-fix choices baked in; option (b) replan a new 350M from scratch incorporating everything learned since (β-fix, NorMuon, Engram v2, possibly Frac-connections, updated recipe defaults, possibly a different MoR router config, LoRA/PEFT for the post-pretrain stages, and — if either clears the gate — GDN-2 or OSDN). Glenn leaning toward (b). Any recall_eval-on-350M-MoR-checkpoint work and the deferred KD-on-350M decision fold into this same decision, whichever path is chosen.</li>
  <li>Build a fair 350M depth-1 baseline (same recipe: delta_rule+decay_gate+mqar_aug, no router) — none exists yet; <code>phase3_350M_kd</code> is not a rigorous comparison. Relevant to whichever 350M path (a)/(b) above gets chosen.</li>
