    </ol>
    Ordering rationale: PMA first so refines start from a stabler base; refines before SFT so the pretrain-shape knobs (recall, sparsity) settle before instruction-following is trained on top; LoRA replaces full-finetune from step 5 onward because the SFT/DPO/brain-mod stages benefit most (small artifacts, tiny VRAM, swappable skills for the demo).</li>
</ol>

<h2>New Architectural Ideas (reshuffled 2026-07-12 after 3 nulls)</h2>
<p class="updated">Post-Test-2 architectural-ceiling verdict promotes <b>Sparse Delta Memory (A)</b> to top strategic priority — it's the only candidate that attacks state <em>capacity</em>, not the write rule. GDN-2/OSDN (B/C) remain worthwhile recall levers but are gated on an OOM fix (grad-checkpointing on the sequential loop). LoRA (D) unblocked from base-ckpt search. Frac-connections closed negative and rotated to Recent Completions. Confirmed items (NorMuon, Engram v2) also in Recent Completions.</p>
<ol type="A">
  <li><b>Sparse Delta Memory (SDM)</b> — <b>TOP PRIORITY, PHASE 2 LANDED &amp; CPU-VERIFIED 2026-07-12; PHASE 3 BLOCKED on 5080 chain finish</b> — <i>arXiv:2607.07386, Meta FAIR, Jul 8 2026</i>. Replaces the dense (d_v × d_k) key-value outer product with sparse reads/writes to a large explicit memory bank (N slots, PKM-addressed), scaling linear-RNN state capacity orders of magnitude under an isoFLOP/iso-param constraint. Paper's 8B ref: state ~7.96B params (111% of non-embedding), RULER 50.2% vs GDN 34.2%. <b>Why now:</b> Test 2's null (2026-07-12) settled that low-N recall is architecturally capped &mdash; the write rule (GDN-2/OSDN) can't fix it, only more state can. Sparse addressing also fits the spiking-sparsity narrative (auditability pillar).
    <br><b>Landed 2026-07-12:</b> <a href="sdm_design.md"><code>docs/sdm_design.md</code></a> &mdash; full paper mapping into our layout (M ∈ R^(N×d), PKM outer-sum addressing in O(√N), per-slot gated delta write on top-W indices, read from top-R indices), composition matrix vs existing wins (beta_floor / decay_gate / NorMuon / Engram v2 all compose; GDN-2/OSDN are mutually exclusive as same-M variants), sizing plan (30M screen: N=1024, W=R=4, ~6M added; 500M restart: N=16384, W=R=8, ~165M added), 8-phase build plan, risk register (dead slots, sparse-scatter grads, autocast top-k determinism, recurrent_depth interaction).
    <br><b>Landed 2026-07-12 late-morning:</b> phase 2 &mdash; <code>src/sdm.py</code> built with <code>PKMIndex</code> (outer-sum + per-sub top-M candidate + global top-K, O(K&middot;&radic;N)) and <code>SparseSDMMemory</code> (M_0 zero-init + separate write/read PKM heads; reuses block β/α/v so <code>beta_floor</code> telemetry keeps working). Fracconn-style CPU verification 22/22 in <code>temp/test_sdm.py</code>: PKM outer-sum invariant + top-K vs dense oracle at K&isin;{1,4,8}; SDM zero-init exact zero (safe residual drop-in); β=0 closed-form; α=0 fast-forget correctness; grad flows through M_0 + both PKM heads; determinism; autocast bf16 finiteness; slot-selection telemetry populated. The design doc's "dense_all_slots reduces to gated delta" claim was corrected as aspirational-and-false (per-slot (N,d) vs delta's (d_v,d_k) shared outer product; different memories structurally, not special cases).
    <br><b>Next (phase 3, blocked on 5080 chain finish):</b> <code>src/model.py</code> wiring behind <code>sdm_enabled: false</code>, mutually-exclusive guards vs gdn2/osdn/vector_beta/titans/n_heads&gt;1, block <code>init_state()</code> method for the (B,N,d) shape, config plumbing in <code>build_model</code> + <code>recall_eval.build()</code>. Then bit-exact flag-off state_dict + forward check before any GPU. Then a 30M A/B (byte-identical to GDN-2/OSDN control except SDM flags). Deferred deliberately so 5080 seed1 doesn't re-import a modified model.py and contaminate the seed0/seed1 A/B fairness the 500M restart decision depends on &mdash; same GDN-2/OSDN discipline.</li>

  <li><b>GDN-2 — erase/write decoupling in the delta rule</b> (<b>BUILT 2026-07-11, UNBLOCKED 2026-07-12 via grad-ckpt, queued on 5090 follow-on chain</b>) — <i>arXiv:2605.22791, NVLabs</i>. The same paper whose β-collapse diagnosis gave us <code>beta_floor</code>; the floor is a patch, this is the actual fix. Standard gated delta uses a single scalar to control two different things — how much old content to erase (key side) and how much new to commit (value side). GDN-2 splits them into a channel-wise erase gate <code>b_t = σ(W_b x)</code> and channel-wise write gate <code>w_t = σ(W_w x)</code>, with channel-wise decay <code>D_t = diag(α_t)</code>. State update (their layout): <code>S_t = (I − k_t(b_t⊙k_t)ᵀ)D_t S_{t−1} + k_t(w_t⊙v_t)ᵀ</code>; reduces to KDA when the gates tie to a scalar and to Gated DeltaNet when decay also collapses. <b>Why it fits:</b> targets exactly the multi-key retrieval our MQAR refines couldn't move, and the write gate <code>w_t</code> carries the <code>beta_floor</code> stability lesson forward as a per-channel floor.
    <br><b>Landed 2026-07-11:</b>
    <ul>
      <li><code>src/model.py</code> — <code>gdn2_enabled: false</code> flag; when on, adds <code>W_b, W_w, W_fdecay</code> (Linear(N,d_mem)) and <code>gdn2_a</code> (Param(d_mem)). Sequential memory-loop kernel inside <code>forward_sequence</code> (LIF stays FPT-parallel).</li>
      <li><code>src/delta_chunked.py</code> — <code>gdn2_sequential</code> reference oracle (verified: reduces exactly to <code>delta_sequential_gated</code> with tied scalar gates).</li>
      <li><code>src/train_gpu.py</code> + <code>scripts/recall_eval.py</code> — config plumbing back-ported.</li>
      <li><code>configs/screen_gdn2_osdn_ab_control_30m.yaml</code> + <code>configs/screen_gdn2_ab_treatment_30m.yaml</code> (shared depth-1 recipe, <code>mqar_aug: true</code>, differ only in <code>gdn2_enabled</code>).</li>
      <li><b>Verified (CPU):</b> 16/16 checks pass in <code>temp/test_gdn2_osdn.py</code> — oracle reduce-to-baseline, flag-off state_dict identical + forward bit-identical, flag-on trains with finite grads, guards fire (needs <code>delta_rule</code>, mutually exclusive with OSDN, rejects <code>n_heads&gt;1</code>).</li>
    </ul>
    <b>Test / gate:</b> 30M A/B (control + this treatment), val_ppl win &gt; ~1.5 ppl noise floor <em>and</em> memory-Δ/recall_eval lift, then seed1 confirm before baking into the next 500M.
    <br><b>OOM finding &rarr; FIX (2026-07-12 01:00):</b> the sequential memory-loop's autograd tape was ~50 GB at batch=4. <b>Fixed same session</b> via grad-checkpointing: extracted <code>_gdn2_chunk_fwd</code> module-level helper, wrapped in <code>torch.utils.checkpoint(use_reentrant=False)</code> per chunk of 32 timesteps (~16x memory reduction, ~2x compute hit during backward). Verified in <code>temp/test_gdn2_osdn_gradckpt.py</code> (8/8 checks + 16/16 original oracle checks): train-mode-with-ckpt is <em>bit-exact</em> to eval-mode-no-ckpt on both forward and grad-signature, peak VRAM measurably lower. Treatment restored to <code>batch=32/grad_accum=1</code>.
    <br><b>RUNNING 2026-07-12 09:30:</b> 30M treatment ~70% through, val_ppl already beating the control's <em>final</em> number. Mid-run eval showed an anomalous memory-Δ +313.65 (~3&times; anything else measured) — investigated: ablation path correctly skips the GDN-2 branch (not a code bug); NVIDIA's own reference init (<code>github.com/NVlabs/GatedDeltaNet-2</code>) differs from ours (no-bias Xavier-uniform gates vs our biased-slow-decay init), but our own plain-gated-delta control had already hit +211 memory-Δ on the same slow-decay bias at seed1 — so +313 reads as real per-channel-gating signal amplifying existing seed variance, not a broken measurement. Run continuing; init-matching A/B banked as a follow-up only if this screen wins.</li>

  <li><b>OSDN — online preconditioned delta rule</b> (<b>BUILT 2026-07-11, UNBLOCKED 2026-07-12 via same grad-ckpt fix, queued on 5090 follow-on chain</b>) — <i>arXiv:2605.13473</i>. Adds a diagonal preconditioner <code>d_t</code> to the delta write: error is read with the plain key, but the rank-1 write goes along the preconditioned key <code>k̃_t = d_t ⊙ k_t</code>. Crucially <code>d_t</code> is <b>not a learned parameter</b> — it is online-adapted per step by a closed-form hypergradient (<code>d_{t+1} = d_t + (ηβ/‖k‖²)(1 − β⟨d_t,k²⟩)k²</code>, boxed to [0.5,2.0], η=0.003), so it adds ~zero params and only a cheap per-step vector state. Reported <b>+32% in-context recall at 340M</b> (near our scale). <b>Why it fits:</b> pure recall lever, orthogonal to gating (composes with our existing <code>decay_gate</code> + <code>beta_floor</code>), very small code change. Because <code>d_t</code> depends only on keys, a chunked kernel can later precompute it in one pass.
    <br><b>Landed 2026-07-11:</b>
    <ul>
      <li><code>src/model.py</code> — <code>osdn_enabled: false</code> flag; when on, adds <b>zero learned params</b> (verified) — <code>d_t</code> is online state, reset per forward-sequence window. Sequential memory-loop kernel with in-loop preconditioner update.</li>
      <li><code>src/delta_chunked.py</code> — <code>osdn_sequential</code> reference oracle (verified: reduces exactly to <code>delta_sequential_gated</code> with η=0; diverges as expected with η&gt;0).</li>
      <li><code>configs/screen_osdn_ab_treatment_30m.yaml</code> (shares the same depth-1 control as GDN-2; differs only in <code>osdn_enabled</code>). Paper-default hyperparameters (<code>osdn_eta: 0.003, osdn_dmin: 0.5, osdn_dmax: 2.0</code>).</li>
      <li>Same 16/16 verification as GDN-2 above.</li>
    </ul>
    <b>Test / gate:</b> same bar as GDN-2 (val_ppl win + memory-Δ/recall lift, then seed1 confirm). Same grad-ckpt fix applied (<code>_osdn_chunk_fwd</code> helper carries M <em>and</em> the online preconditioner <code>d</code> across chunk boundaries). Both queued sequentially on the 5090 follow-on chain (GDN-2 first, OSDN second, LoRA SFT third). If both clear at 30M seed0+seed1, they compose (test the combination at 90M before the 500M bake).</li>

  <li><b>LoRA/PEFT adapters</b> (<b>BUILT &amp; TESTED 2026-07-10, pre-queued for a free 5080 slot</b>) — low-rank adapters for SFT/DPO/brain-mod stages instead of full finetune: faster training, tiny swappable "skill mods," lower VRAM.
    <br><b>Landed:</b>
    <ul>
      <li><code>src/lora.py</code> — <code>LoRALinear</code> (zero-init B = exact no-op at injection, frozen base), <code>inject_lora</code> (targets delta-rule W_q/W_k/W_v by default; skips degenerate 1-row gate matrices), adapter-only save/load, <code>merge_lora</code> (fold-in for zero-overhead inference / brain-mod bake).</li>
      <li><code>src/train_sft.py</code> + <code>src/train_dpo.py</code> — additive wiring behind <code>lora_enabled: false</code> default. When on: adapters trained via a single AdamW (Muon is wrong for low-rank factors), full ckpt for resume + a tiny <code>adapter_step_*.pt</code> swappable artifact. DPO adapts only the policy; the frozen base is the reference.</li>
      <li><code>scripts/merge_lora_ckpt.py</code> — bake base + adapter → plain merged ckpt (chains LoRA-SFT → merge → LoRA-DPO → merge → demo).</li>
      <li><code>configs/sft_30m_lora.yaml</code> — ready to run.</li>
      <li><b>Verified (CPU):</b> injection count, bit-identical no-op@init, adapter-only grads, save/load/merge round-trip against the real <code>SpikingHebbianLM</code>, and the full SFT→merge chain reproduces the adapted output.</li>
    </ul>
    <b>Next:</b> run <code>sft_30m_lora.yaml</code> on a free 5080 slot to validate vs full-finetune SFT. Post-pretrain stage — does NOT gate the 350M or the next 500M.</li>

  <li><b>Curriculum warmup</b> (secondary — data-side, zero arch risk, fills idle 5080 slots) — <i>arXiv:2506.11300</i>. Easy-to-hard data ordering for the first ~10% of tokens, reported sustained ~3.5% gains as a pretrain warmup; difficulty signals = compression ratio / lexical diversity / readability. Orthogonal to every arch change, cheap to screen. <b>Test:</b> 30M A/B on ordering alone, seed1 before adopting.</li>

  <li><b>EBM-lite energy/verifier head</b> (post-pretrain, exploratory) — scalar <code>E(context, candidate)</code> trained contrastively to rerank/reject incoherent continuations; targets hallucination/coherence/robustness. NOT full equilibrium-prop (that would rewrite the whole backprop+Muon+KD pipeline — rejected as impractical).
    <br><b>Scoping note landed 2026-07-10:</b> <code>docs/ebm_lite_design.md</code> — recommends <b>scope now, don't build yet</b>. Post-pretrain inference-time verifier (like LoRA, does NOT gate the 350M). Attaches like the existing <code>confidence_head</code>; negatives = base self-samples + mismatched contexts; loss = margin/InfoNCE. Best home = learned scorer for self-refine critique and/or MTP spec-decode reranking (reuses existing machinery, becomes a demo capability). <b>Hard gate: commit to a pre-registered win condition (ranking-accuracy AUC or best-of-N lift vs base-logprob baseline) BEFORE any code.</b> Implementation-ready doc (2026-07-10): concrete head, per-problem Bradley–Terry loss, EORM data recipe, reference repo to port (<code>github.com/ericjiang18/EnergyORM</code>, arXiv:2505.14999).</li>

  <li><b>Explore activation alternatives: PowLU (and possibly xSwiGLU)</b> (banked, low priority) — researched 2026-07-10, appropriate for a spare 5080 window after the current gates clear. PowLU (arXiv:2605.25704) targets SwiGLU's quadratic-term training instability directly, most relevant if our spiking/delta-rule setup ever shows a stability issue NorMuon/<code>beta_floor</code> don't fix. xSwiGLU / expanded gating range (arXiv:2405.20768) is a near-zero-cost single-scalar widening of the gate, lowest-risk cheap screen.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Recent Completions (this week)</h2>
<p class="updated">Recent completions kept here for quick reference. Older completions and the full rejected-candidates list live in <a href="to-do-archive.html">to-do-archive.html</a>.</p>
<ul>
  <li class="done"><b>F7 landed &amp; exceeded scope: GDN-2 dense chunked-WY kernel, 500M restart named 500M_v3 (2026-07-15).</b> Investigated the fla.ops.gdn2 Triton kernel for the GDN-2 speedup (F7); found its hard 256 key-headdim cap forces block-diagonal multi-head splitting that COLLAPSES MQAR recall as head-count rises (H=1 dense 91% N=16 accuracy &rarr; H=4 70% &rarr; H=8 11% &rarr; H=16 8%, a cliff after H=4 &mdash; confirmed architectural not a fixable kernel-precision bug via same-weights loop-vs-kernel isolation). Rejected the fla kernel; instead derived and built an EXACT chunked-parallel GEMM reformulation of the dense-state recurrence (<code>gdn2_chunked_dense</code> in <code>src/delta_chunked.py</code>, GLA-style two-level anchored gating, all exponentials &le;1). Three real bugs found+fixed via actual training runs (not just synthetic parity): autocast silently downcasting internal fp32 GEMMs to bf16 (NaN by step 100) &mdash; hard-disabled autocast inside the fn; a midpoint-shift exp factorization overflowing/zeroing on trained fast-decay channels &mdash; rewritten as the two-level anchored form; <code>unit_lower_inv</code> Neumann-doubling numerically blowing up on trained MQAR-correlated keys &mdash; swapped to <code>torch.linalg.solve_triangular</code>. Wired behind <code>gdn2_chunked</code> config flag (default <code>true</code>) in <code>model.py</code>/<code>train_gpu.py</code>; the loop stays as fallback, <code>use_fla_kernel</code> path stays available but not recommended. <b>30M validation CONFIRMED:</b> val_ppl 77.03 vs dense-loop champion's 76.98, memory-Δ +299.70 vs +304.54, MQAR recall 89/38/19% (N=16/32/64) vs champion's 91/38/12% &mdash; full recall preserved, at <b>~23x the champion's training speed</b> (23.2k vs ~1k tok/s). <b>Decision: the 500M restart is named <code>500M_v3</code> and its config MUST set <code>gdn2_chunked: true</code></b> (preserves full dense d_mem=2048 state; eliminates GDN-2's ~2x pretraining tax without the fla kernel's recall collapse). See <code>neuro_gdn2_dense_chunked.md</code>.</li>
  <li class="done"><b>Code-review pass + SDM phase 2 built &amp; CPU-verified (2026-07-12 late-morning).</b> Deep architecture/roadmap review produced 11 findings; 4 applied this session as safe additive changes alongside running chains. <b>F3</b>: β pre-floor <code>at_floor_frac</code> telemetry (restores the collapse-pressure signal &mdash; under <code>beta_floor: 0.1</code> the standing "dead_frac climbing" rule can NEVER trigger because β &ge; floor by construction; journey §36's "revived by refine" claim was circular). <b>F11</b>: α clamp-boundary <code>hi_frac</code>/<code>lo_frac</code> telemetry (analog silent-failure of decay-gate freeze). <b>F10</b>: <code>recall_eval.build()</code> missing kwargs added (beta, eta, ponder_*, recurrent_deep_supervision, recurrent_pass_weights) &mdash; same silent-wrong-arch class already bitten twice. <b>F4</b>+<b>F6</b>: SDM design-doc amendments &mdash; 500M's fla kernel splits d_mem into 8&times;256&sup2; diagonal blocks (~8&times; less effective per-layer state than dense 4.19M/layer &mdash; strengthens the SDM thesis and the sizing table needs to compare against *effective* state); GDN-2/OSDN reframed as per-slot write-rule variants of SDM, NOT mutually exclusive. <b>SDM phase 2 built:</b> <code>src/sdm.py</code> with <code>PKMIndex</code> + <code>SparseSDMMemory</code>, 22/22 CPU checks pass in <code>temp/test_sdm.py</code>. <b>Phase 3 deferred</b> to protect 5080 seed1 A/B fairness. Findings not applied this session (queued): F1 (Muon 1-row-gate degeneration), F2 (Engram bucket routing), F5 (add SDM to 500M restart trigger), F8 (confidence-gated replay), F9 (dtype match if GDN-2 promotes).</li>
  <li class="done"><b>30M NorMuon+Engram-v2 combined bake — CONFIRMED both seeds, promoted to 90M (2026-07-12).</b> First-ever test of the two 350M-gate wins run together: seed0 val_ppl 79.06 vs control 81.87 (&minus;2.8 ppl); seed1 val_ppl 68.22 vs control 80.03 (&minus;11.8 ppl). Both seeds beat control in the same direction &mdash; clears the seed-noise bar that MoE Phase 1, Engram v1, and uniform recurrent-depth all failed. <b>The two wins compose without interfering.</b> Promoted to <code>bake_normuon_engramv2_combined_90m.yaml</code> (+seed1 variant), launched on the 5080 (see Currently Running). If 90M confirms, this is the last precursor before the 500M restart plan. See <code>neuro_combined_bake.md</code>.</li>
  <li class="done"><b>500M post-pretrain — Test 2 verdict: NULL, architectural-ceiling CONFIRMED (2026-07-12).</b> <code>mqar_refine_500m_strong</code> ran to completion (5300M tok in 7.05h, 4&times; budget + 6&times; MQAR weight vs the original refine). <code>recall_eval</code> vs the sparsity_refine_500m baseline at N=4/8/16/30: accuracies 44/36/20/14% vs 45/36/20/14% (identical); mean rank 3560/3949/4738/6642 vs 3571/3958/4531/6699 (fluctuations within noise, no consistent direction). <b>Settles budget-vs-architectural-ceiling decisively: the low-N recall null is architectural, not budget.</b> MQAR-refine is a dead pipeline stage for this model class. This is the finding that promotes <b>Sparse Delta Memory to top strategic priority</b> (idea A). See <code>neuro_500m_post_pretrain_refine_null.md</code>.</li>
  <li class="done"><b>Frac-connections A/B — NULL, closed negative (2026-07-12).</b> Treatment final val_ppl <b>88.54</b> vs control 86.69 (<b>&minus;1.85 ppl</b>, well past the ~1.5 ppl noise floor). Memory-Δ higher (+46.95) but overall model worse. Wiring proven inert at flag=off (control matched NorMuon-control byte-for-byte), so this is a real null on the mechanism. Third confirmed instance of the project's "resid-add trick doesn't lift LM quality at 30M" pattern. Full hyper-connections stays rejected. Design doc <code>docs/hyperconnections_design.md</code> retained as historical reference.</li>
  <li class="done"><b>GDN-2/OSDN treatments blocked on OOM (2026-07-12).</b> Sequential memory-loop's autograd tape (T=512, d_mem=1024, 2 layers) is ~50 GB even at batch=4; spills to WDDM shared memory on the 5090 (32 GB), no first step in ~10 min. Not a config problem &mdash; a structural memory issue. Fix path: gradient checkpointing on the per-timestep loop (or halve block_size on treatments only, breaking fairness slightly). Screens paused until fix lands.</li>
  <li class="done"><b>Overnight chain extended 2026-07-12 01:00 with follow-on</b> (<code>temp/chain_5090_followon.sh</code>): waits for the primary chain, then runs (3) GDN-2 treatment 30M &rarr; (4) OSDN treatment 30M &rarr; (5) LoRA SFT 30M. Adds ~5-6h of 5090 work overnight. All three items unblocked earlier in the same session (see below).</li>
  <li class="done"><b>GDN-2 / OSDN grad-checkpointing landed 2026-07-12.</b> Sequential memory loops factored into module-level helpers <code>_gdn2_chunk_fwd</code> / <code>_osdn_chunk_fwd</code>, wrapped with <code>torch.utils.checkpoint(use_reentrant=False)</code> at <code>chunk=32</code>. Cuts peak activation memory ~16&times; (T=512 → C=32) for a ~2&times; backward compute hit. 8/8 new checks + 16/16 original oracle checks pass in <code>temp/test_gdn2_osdn_gradckpt.py</code>: train-mode-with-ckpt is bit-exact to eval-mode-no-ckpt on both forward and grad-signature. Treatment configs restored to <code>batch=32/grad_accum=1</code> (matches control). <b>GDN-2 and OSDN screens unblocked.</b></li>
